          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Symbolically, a bill of divorcement signifies God's judicial rupture or suspension of the covenant-marriage with unfaithful Israel because of spiritual adultery, historically expressed in rejection, exile, or scattering.\",\"book_summary\":\"Divorce signifies a severed covenant and the wife's expulsion, corresponding to exile and scattering; the book additionally distinguishes treacherous divorce of a genuine covenant wife from separation out of a union God did not join.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Covenant rupture and judicial separation of unfaithful Israel, expressed through exile or rejection.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A severed covenant followed by sending away the wife, symbolizing exile and scattering.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book adds an explicit boundary between wrongful dissolution of a valid, God-joined covenant and legitimate separation from an illicit union, apparently using Malachi 2 and Ezra 10 respectively. The consensus recognizes literal legal and ethical divorce questions but does not make this valid-union versus never-joined-union distinction part of the symbol's definition.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the symbolic center: both identify divorce with covenant severance and Israel's resulting expulsion or exile. The only material difference is the book's classification of some acts of putting away as separation from an invalid union rather than treacherous divorce.\",\"rationale\":\"The central referent is preserved almost exactly: covenant severance, sending away, and exile correspond to the consensus's covenant rupture, judicial separation, and exile or scattering. Because the book expressly introduces a substantive condition governing which marital separations count as treacherous divorce, it refines the category's boundary rather than replacing its core.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"A MATCH verdict is plausible because the added distinction concerns literal marriage ethics rather than the primary prophetic symbol, and the consensus already acknowledges diverse legal uses. Nevertheless, the book states the distinction as part of the entry and supports it through the contrast between Ezra 10 and Malachi 2, making it a real boundary refinement.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
